course: dictionary
lesson: 4967
title: Words that look alike
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words look and sound alike but mean very different things. Taffy says «inciting» and «incitement» when she means exciting and excitement. These are funny child's mix-ups, and they show why context matters: the sentence tells you what the writer means.
explain: Incitement means stirring people up to do something bad, violent or against the law. Excitement is a happy, buzzing feeling. The spellings are close, but the meanings are not. Ask yourself: is this about feeling thrilled, or about pushing people into trouble?
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. If Taffy meant excitement, «I felt incitement before my birthday» would not sound right. Reading closely helps you catch a word that does not fit.
fact: Incitement is a noun, and it is usually uncountable: we say «incitement to violence».
fact: Incitement means the act of encouraging or stirring people up to do something bad, violent or against the law.
fact: In law, incitement is the crime of urging people to commit a crime or violence. In the UK, «incitement to racial hatred» is against the law.
fact: Incitement can also mean something that pushes people to act: a cause or reason.
fact: Incitement and excitement look alike, but excitement is a happy, lively feeling, so do not swap them.
exercise: «The leader's speech was incitement to violence.» What does incitement mean here? | *Urging people to commit violence | A sudden rainstorm | A small, quiet room | A happy, buzzing feeling
exercise: Taffy says «incitement» when she means excitement. Why is she wrong? | *Incitement means stirring people up to do something bad, not a happy feeling | Incitement and excitement are spelled the same | Incitement means a happy feeling, just like excitement | Incitement is a kind of food
exercise: Which sentence uses incitement correctly? | *«The judge said the man was guilty of incitement to violence.» | «Taffy felt incitement when the bell rang for playtime.» | «The cat had incitement for its dinner.»
test: «The leader's words were a clear incitement to riot.» What does incitement mean? | *Stirring people up to do something bad, like rioting | A happy feeling at a birthday party | A long, slow walk by the sea
test: Which word is closest in meaning to incitement, as in stirring people up to do wrong? | *encouragement to do wrong | calm | rest | joy
exam: Taffy wrote «I felt incitement before my birthday.» Which word should she use instead? | *excitement | thought | incitement | surprise
